    "created_at": "2024-05-08T08:01:59Z",
    "updated_at": "2024-05-08T08:01:59Z",
    "body": "> This would create a problem with versioning if the page has references to dynamically loaded content. For example, with AngularJS it might have conditional ng-include=\"my-template.html\". So if the user clicks on something - it will attempt to load that HTML from the server. And if we release a new version where that template is removed - it will result in 404.\r\n\r\nDon't you have this problem already without BFCache? If:\r\n\r\n-  I load the page\r\n- you release a new version\r\n- I click\r\n\r\nit will get a 404. Obviously BFCache extends the effective lifetime of pages, so it makes this problem worse but the real cause seems to be the lack of versioning and immediately deleting all of the old resources when you deploy changes.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
